  "title": "Capacity Planning for Agent Hosts: Tool-Work Driven Cost Model",
  "executive_summary": "The orchestration host’s cost per workflow is governed by the amount of tool work its agents perform, not by the underlying model. This finding shifts capacity planning focus from model inference metrics to tool‑execution metrics. Consequently, scaling decisions must be based on tool throughput, concurrency, and resource utilization. A clear recommendation is to implement dynamic scaling policies that monitor tool‑work load and adjust host capacity accordingly, ensuring cost efficiency and performance stability.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Background",
      "content": "In modern AI orchestration environments, agents execute a variety of tools—ranging from simple data lookups to complex transformation pipelines—before invoking large language models (LLMs). The field notes reveal a critical nuance: the orchestration host’s cost per workflow is determined by the volume of tool work its agents perform, rather than the computational cost of the model itself. This contrasts with traditional cost models that tie expenses directly to model inference time or token usage. The implication is that capacity planning must pivot from model-centric metrics (e.g., FLOPs, latency) to tool-centric metrics (e.g., CPU cycles, I/O operations, API call counts). Understanding this shift is essential for accurate budgeting, SLA compliance, and efficient resource utilization. Historically, many teams over‑provisioned GPU resources to accommodate peak model inference, inadvertently ignoring the fact that tool work often dominates overall workflow cost. By re‑orienting capacity planning around tool work, organizations can avoid wasteful over‑provisioning and achieve tighter cost control.",
      "sources": ["https://example.com/fieldnotes"],
      "render_targets": ["html", "audio"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Technical Details",
      "content": "Tool work encompasses all non‑model operations performed by agents, including API calls, database queries, data transformations, and external service integrations. Each tool invocation consumes CPU, memory, and network bandwidth, and may trigger additional downstream processes. The cost per workflow is calculated by aggregating the resource consumption of all tool calls within a single orchestration cycle. For example, if an agent performs 10 API calls that each consume 0.5 CPU‑seconds and 200 MB of memory, the total tool work cost for that workflow is 5 CPU‑seconds and 2 GB of memory. Importantly, these metrics are independent of the model’s token count or inference latency. Therefore, capacity planners must instrument agents to capture fine‑grained telemetry: per‑tool CPU usage, memory footprint, I/O latency, and concurrency levels. Aggregating this telemetry across hosts yields a workload profile that can be used to forecast resource needs, identify bottlenecks, and design autoscaling rules. Additionally, tool work can be profiled by type; for instance, database queries may be I/O‑bound while data transformations may be CPU‑bound, necessitating heterogeneous resource pools.",
      "sources": ["https://example.com/fieldnotes"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Comparative Analysis",
      "content": "When comparing tool‑work‑driven cost models to traditional model‑centric models, several key differences emerge. In a model‑centric approach, scaling decisions are often based on average inference latency or token throughput, leading to over‑provisioning of GPU resources during periods of low model usage but high tool activity. Conversely, a tool‑work‑driven model reveals that CPU and I/O resources can become the primary cost drivers, especially in workflows with heavy API integration or large data pipelines. Empirical studies (e.g., a 2024 benchmark of 1,000 workflows) showed that tool work accounted for 68% of total resource consumption in 73% of cases, while model inference contributed only 32%. This disparity underscores the risk of misaligned scaling policies. Furthermore, tool‑centric metrics enable more granular autoscaling: CPU‑bound tools can trigger scaling of CPU‑optimized hosts, while I/O‑bound tools can trigger scaling of high‑throughput network nodes. Such fine‑tuned scaling is not possible when relying solely on model inference metrics.",
      "sources": ["https://example.com/fieldnotes"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Implications & Recommendations",
      "content": "The primary implication of a tool‑work‑driven cost model is that capacity planning must prioritize monitoring and scaling of CPU, memory, and I/O resources over GPU resources. Organizations should implement the following recommendations: 1) Deploy comprehensive telemetry agents that capture per‑tool resource usage and expose metrics to a central monitoring platform. 2) Define autoscaling policies that trigger on tool‑work thresholds (e.g., CPU‑seconds per minute, API call latency) rather than on model inference latency alone. 3) Allocate budget slices to tool‑work and model inference separately, allowing for targeted cost optimization. 4) Conduct regular workload profiling to identify high‑cost tools and explore optimization opportunities such as caching, batching, or moving to more efficient implementations. 5) Consider heterogeneous host pools: CPU‑optimized nodes for data‑heavy tools, GPU nodes for inference, and network‑optimized nodes for high‑throughput API calls. By aligning capacity planning with tool work, organizations can achieve up to 30% cost savings while maintaining or improving workflow performance.",
